{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Common.PeriodicDense","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Common.PeriodicDense","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Circle","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Common.dense_primaryPole_compl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Common.dense_primaryPole_compl","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Common/PeriodicDense.lean#L97-L134","doc":"**The primary pole lattice complement is dense** (the `ib`-period companion of\n`dense_dualPole_compl`, for the `hib` discreteness input). The zero set of the\nprimary quasi-period prefactor `1 + exp(iπb²)·exp(2πbz)` is where\n`exp(iπb² + 2πbz) = -1`; by `Complex.exp_eq_exp_iff_exists_int` this pins `z` to a\ndiscrete `ℤ`-lattice, so the zero set is countable and its complement — the guard\ndomain of `CyclicDilogPentagon.ratio_ib_periodic` — is dense in `ℂ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Common.dense_dualPole_compl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Common.dense_dualPole_compl","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Common/PeriodicDense.lean#L54-L95","doc":"**The dual pole lattice complement is dense** (the `hibinv` discreteness\ninput, bean `qou-q95i`). The zero set of the dual quasi-period prefactor\n`1 + exp(2π(z + i/2b)/b)` is exactly where `exp(2π(z + i/2b)/b) = -1`; by\n`Complex.exp_eq_exp_iff_exists_int` this pins `z` to the discrete lattice\n`{ (πi + n·2πi)·b/(2π) − i/2b : n ∈ ℤ }`, so the zero set is **countable** and its\ncomplement — the set on which `CyclicDilogPentagon.ratio_ibinv_periodic`'s guard\n`hpre` holds — is **dense** in `ℂ`. Combined with `periodic_of_continuous_of_eqOn_dense`\n(and the `qou-2cvs` continuity endowment) this discharges the `hibinv` input of\n`FaddeevRigidityReduction` from the everywhere-periodicity side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Common.dense_compl_of_countable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Common.dense_compl_of_countable","kind":"theorem","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Common/PeriodicDense.lean#L40-L52","doc":"**A countable subset of `ℂ` has dense complement.** `sᶜ = ⋂_{x ∈ s} {x}ᶜ`, a\ncountable intersection of dense open sets, so it is dense by Baire (`ℂ` is a\ncomplete metric — hence Baire — perfect space). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Common.periodic_of_continuous_of_eqOn_dense","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Common.periodic_of_continuous_of_eqOn_dense","kind":"theorem","line":25,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Common/PeriodicDense.lean#L25-L38","doc":"A continuous `r : ℂ → ℂ` that is `c`-periodic on a **dense** set is\n`c`-periodic everywhere: `r(·+c)` and `r` are continuous and agree on a dense\nset, so they agree. ","signatureChunk":0}]}